//? Title: OneR: one nominal attribute, actual majority rules
//? Subtitle: Original play-tennis candidates, training-only Titanic fitting and frozen fallback
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Derive the empirical best single categorical attribute rule from tiny bucket
counts, then retain the original fourteen-row weather illustration. A separate
native Titanic training/test study distinguishes candidate training error from
held-out prediction, with raw confusion counts and an explicit frozen fallback
for unseen categories.
)

\toc

> Minimize misclassification within each feature value

Let C_jvc count training rows with feature j equal to nominal value v and class
c. For a fixed j, choosing one prediction per value independently minimizes
unweighted training errors:

$$ E_j=\sum_v\left(\sum_c C_{jvc}-\max_c C_{jvc}\right), \qquad j^*=\arg\min_j E_j. $$
\alttext(Each value bucket contributes its size minus its largest class count; the selected attribute has the smallest sum of these errors.)

The native tie policy chooses the smallest integer class within a tied bucket,
and the first original feature among equal candidate errors. An unseen
selected-feature value uses the frozen global training-majority class, again
breaking a class tie by the smallest integer label. Integers are category codes:
their numeric order does not create intervals or distances. This implementation
is the nominal-attribute rule learner; it does not fit continuous-value
discretization or claim performance close to more complex classifiers on
arbitrary data.

> A tiny tied bucket and an unambiguous bucket

For feature values[0,0,1,1] and labels[0,1,1,1], value0 has one example of each
class and chooses0; value1 chooses1. The selected rule has one training error
and global fallback1. Both the diagram and table read the actual bucket tallies
produced during fitting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/one_r_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/one_r.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::one_r_train_trace({{0}, {0}, {1}, {1}}, {0, 1, 1, 1});
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    auto &b = t.candidates[0].buckets;
    d.node("zero", {0, 1.5},
           std::format("Actual value {}\nmajority {} errors {}", b[0].value,
                       b[0].prediction, b[0].errors));
    d.node("one", {0, -1.5},
           std::format("Actual value {}\nmajority {} errors {}", b[1].value,
                       b[1].prediction, b[1].errors));
    d.node("model", {9, 0},
           std::format("Actual selected feature {}\ntotal errors {} fallback {}",
                       t.model.feature, t.model.errors, t.model.default_class));
    d.edge("zero", "model").edge("one", "model");
    std::print("| actual feature value | actual label | actual count | selected bucket prediction | bucket " "errors |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &v : b)
        for (auto[label, count] : v.class_counts)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", v.value, label, count,
                       v.prediction, v.errors);
    d.save_svg("build/one_r_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual feature value | actual label | actual count | selected bucket prediction | bucket errors |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 0 |
\image(figures/one_r_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual tied and unambiguous bucket decisions from one canonical fitting pass.)
\alttext(Value0 breaks its class tie toward0; value1 predicts1. The rule has one training error and fallback class1.)

> Retain the original fourteen-row weather illustration

The original categorical columns are outlook(sunny0,overcast1,rain2),
temperature(hot0,mild1,cool2), humidity(high0,normal1), and windy(false0,true1);
class0 means no play and1 means play. The reusable fixture retains all fourteen
rows, labels and column order.

The report no longer refits four separately rebuilt one-column matrices to
recover candidate errors. The canonical training history already contains each
candidate’s actual bucket counts and error sum. Selecting a feature by those
training errors does not turn its resubstitution accuracy into an independent
estimate of predictive performance. This small didactic table has no held-out
role in the Titanic study below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/one_r_weather.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/one_r.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto data = dm::algorithms::one_r_weather_data();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::one_r_train_trace(data.features, data.labels);
    std::vector<double> errors;
    std::print("Actual selected feature: {}. Training errors: {} of {}. Training accuracy: {:.9f}. Frozen " "fallback: {}.\n\n",
               data.names[t.model.feature], t.model.errors, t.model.total,
               t.model.accuracy(), t.model.default_class);
    std::print("| original candidate | actual training errors | actual value buckets |\n| --- | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (auto &c : t.candidates) {
        errors.push_back(c.errors);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", data.names[c.feature], c.errors,
                   c.buckets.size());
    }
    std::print("\n| selected feature value | actual class prediction |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto[value, label] : t.model.value_to_class)
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", value, label);
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(errors, data.names, "training errors");
    p.title("Original weather example: actual candidate errors").x_label(
        "original nominal feature").y_label("actual training misclassifications").save_svg("build/one_r_weather.svg");
}
```
